### The gate headroom is real, and no cheap one-threshold gate reaches it

Read this as a **bound**, not a score. The sample is 16 GSM8K prompts where the base is correct and memory-2m is wrong, plus 4 control prompts that are excluded here; the scored text is the arm's own stored generation, 2176 completion positions in total. It is a selected damage sample, so every number below describes *where the memory hurts*, not a benchmark.

| signal | all off | all on | damage | per-token oracle | gain vs off (m nats/token, 95% CI) | class gate (held-out) | entropy gate (held-out) | residual-norm gate | position gate |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| CE | 0.2662 | 0.2850 | 0.0188 | 0.2377 | +28.43 [+21.28, +35.99] | 0.2666 | 0.2662 | 0.2683 | 0.2657 |
| KL | 0.1051 | 0.1223 | 0.0172 | 0.0906 | +14.55 [+9.37, +21.62] | 0.1051 | 0.1051 | 0.1068 | 0.1099 |

All cells are generated by `scripts/s46_headroom_summary.py` from `results/stage2/s46-oracle-bounds-20261117T090710Z.json` (stamp `20261117T090710Z`) and the forward receipt it names (stamp `20261117T084501Z`). The gain column is the prompt-clustered bootstrap mean of `all_off - value` in millinats per token; the other policy columns are their held-out values (a class set or threshold fitted on one half of the prompts, applied to the other half, pooled).

**What the bound says.** On this damage sample memory-always-on costs 0.0188 nats CE per token over memory-always-off. A per-token oracle, which sees the target token and may choose the branch position by position, reaches 0.2377 nats -- +0.0284 better than switching the memory off entirely (2.51x the net damage). That is the headroom: a gate worth its inference cost has to capture part of this improvement over the *free* policy, not merely reproduce it.

**What the cheap gates actually do.** Every held-out gate here lands at or just above all-off (0.2662): class 0.2666, entropy 0.2662, residual norm 0.2683, position 0.2657. Their gains versus all-off are class -0.40 [-2.02, +1.17], base_entropy -0.01 [-0.01, -0.00], memory_residual_norm -2.12 [-4.39, -0.07], position +0.51 [-1.45, +2.88] millinats per token, and every interval includes zero. In words: a one-threshold gate on a single cheap feature recovers the damage by turning the memory off and captures essentially none of the per-token oracle's gain. The class gate is also unstable across folds (fitted on A it turns on digit, fitted on B it turns on operator), which is why its pooled value hides a fold that is worse than memory-always-on.

**Reading for the next mechanism.** The headroom is not in the class or in a single feature threshold; it is in the per-position choice, and the per-token bound is non-causal. A learned gate therefore has to be trained to predict the *sign* of the residual's local effect from causally available state, and it has to beat all-off on held-out prompts before it can be called a mechanism. KL tells the same story (0.0172 nats damage; oracle gain +0.0145), so the conclusion does not depend on the signal.
